ing a lot of people this  question we've got so much  division so much uh racial injustice  so much anger around it so much  political division  when everyone's so divided what do you  think holds us together  as a country what is it that we what is  it that we share in common as a country  when we've got so much division  along these big issues we are all human  just hope at the end of the day exactly  that we might get  to a better tomorrow but it doesn't it  does it's not going to take just me it  doesn't take one pin it doesn't it takes  all of us  so until we can actually unite that's  when actual change can happen  all right well thank you for taking the  time i appreciate it thank you  and so i'll be posting a weekly update  and if you're interested  please post any comments you have any  questions  and i hope you'll come along for the  journey  [Music]  so frank you are two weeks in you've  done a lot  but i want to just start by talking  about your time in trenton and philly  what are some of the highlights for you  from those trips  rainbow brought together this really  great community of artists who were  there to  paint the walls of this recycling  company and um  we talked a little bit with leon about  the work that he's done in the community  including post george floyd riots about  the painting of the graffiti  work that he's done downtown on boarded  up uh buildings and on some  buildings that had their windows smashed  in uh and um  you know why he did it and you don't  often hear about you know graffiti as a  uh as a way to heal a community and  bring it together but that's what he's  doing and i thought it was pretty  powerful  and can you bring me into that  conversation what are some of the things  that you  talked about that really resonated with  you  frank i'm having trouble hearing you for  some reason i'm so sorry  um but we we are talking with frank  berry as a reminder to our viewers  about his experience traveling the  country he's in an rv  going all the way from new york to san  francisco and i was just asking about  the  graffiti you interviewed frank if we  have sound from you  can you tell us a little bit more about  that  sure uh can you hear me now yes i can  hear you it's perfect  okay so  doing in trenton to try to help the  community heal in the wake of the george  floyd riots through his graffiti and  through this community  of artists that he's brought together  and then from there we went down into  philadelphia  and i met with the leader of a band  called low cut connie  uh and they have really become a  streaming hit uh  in the age of cobin they've built a real  following online and he puts on twice  weekly shows in which he's  putting on this really high energy  performance  and people of all walks of life have  really  uh really gotten into it and he's built  this pretty diverse audience  uh that that is really proud of and that  he's been working  to build for a long time that that  crosses racial and  and all kinds of cultural lines  and it's something that uh is not that  common  in the music industry and i talked with  him about some of the pushback that he's  gotten from the industry  in terms of building that audience and  his commitment to it and  some of the some of the difficulties and  holding together  this diverse fan base uh which includes  lots of uh conservative fans who have  been unhappy with some of the  music that he's done and some of the  things that he's talked about on these  live streams  and moving ahead you had a really kind  of shocking experience in lancaster can  you  tell us what happened while you were  there  sure so when we got to lancaster we  settled in for the night and didn't  realize until the next morning that that  night there was actually a police  shooting  uh downtown and uh it was a police  shooting of a young  man um and  it resulted in protests in front of the  police station  that resulted in the end  with tear gas and rubber bullets being  fired and windows  being smashed at the police department  at the postal  office across the street and at some  local businesses  and so i went down the next morning  to its really quiet streets uh to talk  with some of the people who had been  there about what they saw  and what happened and it sounds like  what started as a peaceful protest grew  out of control  in part because of some people  that i spoke to anyway who said that you  know there were uh  people who were not part of their group  who had really not been part of the  protest that they've been carrying on  since the george floyd  killing and things just got out of  control  but it was another example of um not  just an issue  of a police shooting  but of um of the police response and the  unhappiness with it in terms of the the  use of force  and frank you interviewed multiple uh  constituents in the area about their  experience and i know we played some  sound from that in our video but  uh can you take me into some of those  conversations and what people told you  that they were thinking following that  police thing  i'm sorry master would you mind  repeating that you cut out there yeah of  course no i'm wondering if you can just  tell me a little bit we heard some sound  in that package  about how people reacted to the police  shooting but can you tell me more  about what people told you that their  reactions were  yeah so so people were you know it was  interesting we we ended up talking  less about the shooting than we did  about the police response to it and as  you can imagine  there was uh there have been protests  going on since the george floyd protest  uh the george floyd killing around  i think we might be losing frank so we  are going to give  you all another look at his week you can  find his column  weekly in the bloomberg opinion section  uh but for now let's just give you one  more look at  uh frank's week on the road i'm frank  barry a writer with bloomberg opinion  and i'm taking this rv  across the country from new york city to  san francisco along the lincoln highway  this week the road brought us through  trenton new jersey where i met with the  organizer of a graffiti festival i  talked with him about some of the work  that he's done to create a community of  artists this is jersey fresh jam so this  is our 15th  year and you know a lot of people come  from all over and just to celebrate art  and life  from there we drove south to  philadelphia where i talked with the  leader of a band called low cut connie  which has become a streaming sensation  in the age of covet  [Music]  from there we drove west to lancaster  pennsylvania and the night we were in  lancaster  there was a fatal police shooting  protesters came downtown  and what started as a peaceful protest  ended with tear gas  and rubber bullets being fired the next  morning i met with people who were there  spoke with them about the protest about  the changes they want to see  not just in lancaster but across the  country let me ask you a question  because i've been asking a lot of people  this question we've got so much  division so much racial injustice  so much anger around it so much  political division  when everyone's so divided what do you  think holds us together  as a country what is it that we what is  it that we share in common as a country  when we've got so much division  along these big issues we are all human  just hope exactly that we might get  to a better tomorrow but it doesn't it  does it's not going to take just me it  doesn't take one pin it doesn't it takes  all of us  so until we can actually unite that's  when actual change can happen  all right well thank you for taking the  time i appreciate it thank you  and so i'll be posting a weekly update  and if you're interested  please post any comments you have any  questions and i hope you'll come along  for the journey

